WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.650
SPEAKER: Welcome back.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.260
Today, we're very lucky to be here in the Berkeley Graduate

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.010
School of Journalism and joined by Rebecca Skloot, who

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:14.730
is a visiting writer in residence here.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.330
And Rebecca, of course, is the author of the bestselling and award winning book,

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:21.460
The Immortal Life of Henrietta Lacks.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.120
And it is no exaggeration to say this is the most important book written

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.920
for a public audience in the history of bioethics.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.180
So we're incredibly lucky to be joined by her today.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.110
Welcome, Rebecca.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.193
REBECCA SKLOOT: Thank you.

00:00:33.193 --> 00:00:34.120
Thanks for having me.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.703
SPEAKER: So Rebecca, I know you've been asked this many times,

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:39.410
and of course everybody should read this wonderful book,

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:44.106
but can you give us the short form story of Henrietta Lacks and the HeLa cells?

00:00:44.106 --> 00:00:45.480
REBECCA SKLOOT: Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.200
So where I always like to start is just with HeLa.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.620
So the word HeLa is spelled H-E-L-A. And it's an abbreviation

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.310
for Henrietta's name-- H-e for Henrietta, L-A for Lacks.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:58.000
And Henrietta was a poor, black tobacco farmer

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.710
who grew up in southern Virginia.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:04.629
And in 1951, when she was 30 years old, she was diagnosed with cervical cancer.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:06.890
At this point, she had moved up to Baltimore

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.819
and she went to Johns Hopkins, which was the only hospital where

00:01:10.819 --> 00:01:13.360
she could actually get treated, because she was a black woman

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.880
and this was the era of segregation.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.760
So she was seen in what's called the public ward, which

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.740
was where you went if you were black or poor

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.320
and you couldn't afford to be treated anywhere else.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.480
So she went to Hopkins.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.510
And before treating her cancer, without telling her,

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:33.510
her doctor just cut a little piece of her tumor and put that in a dish.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.070
And he sent that down the hall to George Gey,

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.172
who was the head of tissue culture research at Hopkins.

00:01:39.172 --> 00:01:41.630
And George Gey, along with scientists all around the world,

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.680
had been trying to grow human cells outside the body for decades.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:45.709
And it never worked.

00:01:45.709 --> 00:01:47.750
They could survive a few days, maybe a few weeks,

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.090
but they all died pretty quickly.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:53.510
And for reasons that remained a mystery for decades,

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.420
Henrietta's cells just never died.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.980
So they pretty quickly just started exploding.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.820
They doubled their numbers every 24 hours.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.730
And they went from two dishes to four to eight to 16.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:07.260
And they sort of pretty quickly took over the lab.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:11.760
And when George Gey realized this, he started calling all these scientists

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.670
and saying, I think I have the first immortal human cell

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.620
line, which is what they're called, which means they will just grow

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.410
and divide forever in the lab as long as you keep them warm

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.610
and you feed them and keep them clean.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.880
In response, all of these scientists said, great.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.100
Can we have some?

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.050
And he said yes.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.070
And so he would send these cells to other scientists, who would then

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.570
grow them in their lab and give them to their friends, who'd

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:34.810
give them to their friends.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.240
And Henrietta's cells spread around the world this way,

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.500
very inefficient system for doing this.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.740
And so pretty quickly, these facilities were set up

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.700
for mass producing her cells.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.040
So the giant, industrial sized vats of cultured medium,

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.210
which is what you feed cells, assembly lines for putting them into dishes.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:56.620
And her cells were grown to the tune of about 6 trillion cells a week

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.320
and sent out to laboratories around the world.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.190
So the volume of cells that grew from this one little tiny sample

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.120
is sort of inconceivable.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.210
One scientist estimated that if you could have saved them all

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.230
and put them on a scale, that by today they'd

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.260
weigh more than 15 million metric tons.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.210
And individual cells weigh almost nothing.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:17.570
So it's so hard to wrap your mind around how many cells that is.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:20.830
And they really changed science.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:24.080
They were one of the most important things that happened to medicine.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.124
They very quickly were used to help develop the polio vaccine.

00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:30.540
They went up in the first space missions to see what would

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.770
happen to human cells in zero gravity.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.650
Her cells were the first ever cloned.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.022
Her genes were some of the first ever mapped.

00:03:37.022 --> 00:03:39.980
They were used to create some of our most important cancer medications,

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.450
like Vincristine and Tamoxifen.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.430
The HPV vaccine we have thanks to her cells.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.980
In vitro fertilization, a lot of the technology for that

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:49.390
was developed using her cells.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.170
The list just goes on and on.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:55.470
And Henrietta had no idea that the cells were out there.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.660
And part of what made them so valuable for science

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.229
was that they grew with this intensity and they

00:04:00.229 --> 00:04:01.520
did the same thing in her body.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.680
So her initial tumor, which was about the size of a nickel,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.060
spread to almost every organ in her body within just a few months.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.430
And she died just after her 31st birthday.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:13.970
And she left five kids behind.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:15.720
They didn't know anything about the cells.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:16.902
Neither did her husband.

00:04:16.902 --> 00:04:18.860
And they just sort of went on with their lives.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.730
They lived in poverty in East Baltimore.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.920
They lived pretty difficult lives.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.920
And they just never heard anything about the cells until the 1970s.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.590
So flash forward a few decades.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.580
And these were the early days of gene mapping.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:36.340
And one scientist, Victor McKusick, who's

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.790
sort of known as the grandfather of the Human Genome Project,

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.450
he was this incredible geneticist at Hopkins.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:45.390
He decided to track down Henrietta's kids,

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.890
because he thought if he could get some samples of their DNA,

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.330
then he could essentially test their DNA and use

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.710
that to learn more about HeLa cells.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.750
So he sent a post-doc to call Henrietta's husband one day.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.550
And Henrietta's husband had a fourth grade education.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.840
He didn't know what a cell was.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.230
And the way he understood this fellow was essentially, we've got your wife.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:09.880
She's alive in a laboratory.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.690
Part of her's alive in a laboratory.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:14.680
We've been doing research on her for the last 25 years.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.660
And now, we have to test your kids to see

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.482
if they have the cancer that killed her, none of which

00:05:19.482 --> 00:05:20.940
was what the scientist said at all.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.567
She said we want to look at your kid's HLA markers

00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:25.400
and compare those to the HeLa cells, I mean,

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.430
things that most people wouldn't have understood.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.180
And his reference point, the only cell he'd ever heard of, was a prison cell.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.000
So he was like, so they have part of her or some of her in a cell,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.590
like a prison cell.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:41.280
And part of what happened in that moment was

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.460
that just linguistically, he and the scientist didn't understand each other.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.510
There was this sort of enormous communication breakdown.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:49.590
She was from China.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.250
She hadn't been in the US very long.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.910
She had a very thick accent.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.320
And David Lacks came from southern Virginia.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.540
He spoke with a very thick accent that his kids referred to as the country

00:05:58.540 --> 00:05:59.320
mumble.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.123
He was just like, [MUMBLING].

00:06:01.123 --> 00:06:04.241
It was very hard to understand him until you got used to talking to him.

00:06:04.241 --> 00:06:06.740
So they just sort of literally didn't understand each other,

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:10.610
but also they spoke different languages in terms of science and not science.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:13.634
He really didn't have the basic science education

00:06:13.634 --> 00:06:15.050
to understand what she was saying.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:16.410
And she didn't realize that.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:17.790
So she was very well intentioned.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.100
She was excited to talk to him.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:23.400
She thought HeLa cells were amazing and Henrietta was famous.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:27.380
But it was just this moment of complete communication misfire.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.940
So what he heard was we have to test your kids to see if they have cancer.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:34.540
And for Henrietta's kids, particularly her daughter, this was terrifying.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:37.120
So her daughter was nearing her 30th birthday.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:38.430
This is Deborah Lacks.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.957
And she had sort of lived her life in fear of turning 30,

00:06:40.957 --> 00:06:42.540
because she knew her mother died then.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:43.749
Didn't know why.

00:06:43.749 --> 00:06:45.790
So for her, these scientists coming at this point

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.162
seemed like it confirmed her worst fears.

00:06:48.162 --> 00:06:50.620
So the scientists would come to the house and take samples.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.310
And Deborah was this mixture of terrified

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:56.170
about finding out she had cancer, but also very excited,

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:59.227
because she thought maybe these people can tell her about her mother,

00:06:59.227 --> 00:07:01.060
because she was a toddler when her mom died.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.602
And no one ever really talked about her.

00:07:03.602 --> 00:07:06.310
So the scientists would come to the house and take these samples.

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:10.480
And Deborah would say things like, can you look in these bits of my mother

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.760
that you have growing and tell me what her favorite color was,

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:17.210
and whether she liked to dance, and if she breastfed me.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.540
And she was also very worried that her mother might somehow

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.900
be feeling pain because of this research,

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.162
so she would, as the scientists sort of tried to explain some of the research

00:07:26.162 --> 00:07:29.370
to her, she would say things like, if you're shooting these bits of my mother

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:33.020
up to the moon and injecting them with these chemicals,

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.910
does that somehow hurt her in the afterlife?

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:37.080
Can she rest in peace if you're doing that?

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.600
And she heard something about polio research

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.570
and sort of imagined her mother's spirit experiencing

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:45.880
all of the physical pain of polio in the afterlife.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:48.714
And so she was really worried that she needed to protect her mother.

00:07:48.714 --> 00:07:50.755
And she would ask the scientists these questions.

00:07:50.755 --> 00:07:53.020
And they just had no idea how to respond to her.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.506
One of them gave her a medical school genetics textbook

00:07:55.506 --> 00:07:57.130
and just sort of said, here, read this.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:00.790
This will tell you what you need to know about your mother, which it didn't.

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:02.760
And this went on for years.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.610
And pretty soon, scientists started hearing,

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:06.880
someone has access to HeLa's kids.

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:10.110
And can I have samples of their cells and DNA, too?

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.725
And so pretty soon, their cells started being sent out

00:08:13.725 --> 00:08:16.100
to scientists around the world, just like HeLa cells had.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:19.541
Pretty soon, her sons figured out that people were buying and selling

00:08:19.541 --> 00:08:22.290
these cells and that in addition to all the important science that

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:25.910
happened using them, that they really launched a multibillion dollar

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:26.540
industry.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:30.860
So many of the most important biotech companies that are now out there

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:34.030
are sort of supplying genes and cells and patenting

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:38.159
genes and all those things, started off as this very small company that

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.170
just started selling HeLa cells.

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:41.919
And when they found that out, they started

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:45.260
saying, so if people are buying and selling these things,

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:47.060
essentially where's our cut?

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:50.420
And if our mother's cells were so important to medicine, why can't we

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:51.350
go to the doctor?

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:55.730
Why don't we get access to the medical care that her cells helped create?

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:59.850
And no one had a very good answer to that question for them.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:04.750
And that, in a sense, kept going for many years after that.

